/* Contenido profundo de ob_spanish2 (borrador para revisión del equipo curricular; ver "verify" por unidad). */
window.EL_DEEP = window.EL_DEEP || {};
Object.assign(window.EL_DEEP, {"us:world_language:spanish2:Oct": {"reading": ["Every morning, millions of Spanish speakers begin the day with the same small sequence of actions: me despierto, me levanto, me ducho, me visto. These are reflexive verbs. A verb is reflexive when the subject performs the action on itself, and Spanish shows this with a reflexive pronoun placed next to the verb. The pronouns are me (I), te (you, informal), se (he, she, you formal), nos (we), os (you all, mostly used in Spain) and se (they, you all). In a simple sentence the pronoun comes before the conjugated verb: Ella se levanta a las siete. When the sentence has an infinitive, the pronoun may stay in front of the first verb or attach to the end of the infinitive, so both Me voy a acostar and Voy a acostarme are correct. Compare Lavo el coche (I wash the car) with Me lavo (I wash myself).", "Many daily-routine verbs are also stem-changing verbs, sometimes called boot verbs because the changed forms form a boot shape on a conjugation chart. In empezar (to begin) the e becomes ie: empiezo, empiezas, empieza. In poder (to be able) and dormir (to sleep) the o becomes ue: puedo, duermo. In pedir (to ask for) the e becomes i: pido, pides, pide. The nosotros and vosotros forms keep the original vowel (empezamos, dormimos), so the change happens only inside the boot. Combine these patterns with sequencing words and you can narrate a whole morning: Primero me despierto a las seis. Luego me levanto y me ducho. Antes de desayunar, me visto. Después de desayunar, empiezo mis tareas. Por fin, por la noche, me acuesto y duermo ocho horas. The expressions antes de and después de are followed by an infinitive, not by a conjugated verb, which is why we say después de desayunar and not después de desayuno.", "Language also has a history, and few stories show this as clearly as the story of the Spanish Bible. Casiodoro de Reina was born in Spain around the year 1520. He became a monk in the monastery of San Isidoro del Campo, near Seville, where a group of monks had become deeply interested in reading Scripture and in the ideas of the Protestant Reformation. In the middle of the sixteenth century the Spanish Inquisition was investigating and punishing people suspected of holding such ideas, and around 1557 Reina and several fellow monks left Spain. He lived as an exile and pastor in different cities of Protestant Europe, including places in Germany, England and Switzerland, and he often faced poverty and suspicion. Throughout this time he kept working on a project that took roughly a dozen years: translating the whole Bible into Spanish.", "What made his work remarkable is that Reina translated mainly from the original languages, Hebrew for the Old Testament and Greek for the New Testament, and compared them with earlier translations and Latin versions. Spanish speakers already had portions of the Bible in earlier medieval translations, which were largely based on the Latin Vulgate, and Catholic translators would later produce important Spanish Bibles of their own, such as the work of Felipe Scío in the eighteenth century and of Félix Torres Amat in the nineteenth. Reina's Bible, however, is generally recognized as the first complete Spanish Bible printed from the Hebrew and Greek texts. It was published in Basel, Switzerland, in 1569. People call it the Biblia del Oso, the Bear Bible, because the printer's emblem on the title page shows a bear reaching for a honeycomb in a tree. This text became the foundation of the Reina-Valera tradition, which many Spanish-speaking evangelicals still use today.", "Why does this matter for a student learning Spanish? Because words are never only vocabulary; they carry meaning, memory and relationship. The vocabulary of this unit, la Biblia, la traducción, el idioma, leer and el monje, describes people who believed that every person should be able to hear God's Word in a language the heart understands. When you build your own morning routine in Spanish, you are practicing the same principle: ordinary speech becomes a way to live out faith. A sentence such as Me levanto, luego leo la Biblia and oro is grammar practice, but it is also a habit shared by believers across centuries. The first step of our Gap project asks a related question: who in your own area could use a Spanish Bible or a Christian resource, and what would it take to put it in their hands?"], "glossary": [{"term": "verbo reflexivo", "def": "Reflexive verb: a verb whose action returns to the subject, used with the pronouns me, te, se, nos, os, se (for example, me levanto, 'I get up')."}, {"term": "verbo con cambio de raíz", "def": "Stem-changing verb: a verb whose stem vowel changes in some forms (e-ie, o-ue, e-i), such as empiezo, puedo, duermo, pido."}, {"term": "primero, luego, después, por fin", "def": "Sequencing words meaning 'first, then, afterward, finally', used to order the steps of a routine."}, {"term": "la traducción", "def": "Translation: the process or result of rendering a text from one language into another."}, {"term": "el idioma", "def": "Language, especially as the language of a people; the 'mother tongue' in which someone thinks and prays."}, {"term": "el monje", "def": "Monk: a man who lives in a religious community under shared rules, such as Casiodoro de Reina at San Isidoro del Campo."}, {"term": "la Biblia del Oso", "def": "The Bear Bible: Reina's complete Spanish Bible printed in Basel in 1569, named after the bear on the printer's emblem."}, {"term": "exilio", "def": "Exile: a forced or necessary absence from one's homeland, often for reasons of conscience or safety."}], "practice": [{"q": "Rewrite this sentence correctly and explain the pronoun: 'Yo levanto a las siete.' How would you say it with 'voy a'?", "feedback": "The correct sentence is Me levanto a las siete, because levantarse is reflexive and the pronoun me must match the subject yo. Without the pronoun the sentence would mean 'I lift (something) at seven.' With ir a plus infinitive you can say Me voy a levantar a las siete or Voy a levantarme a las siete. Both are correct because the pronoun may go before the first verb or attach to the infinitive."}, {"q": "Conjugate empezar, poder and dormir in the yo, tú and nosotros forms, and explain which form does not change its stem and why.", "feedback": "The forms are empiezo, empiezas, empezamos; puedo, puedes, podemos; duermo, duermes, dormimos. The nosotros form keeps the original vowel because the stress falls on the ending, not on the stem. This is why these are called boot verbs: only the forms inside the boot (yo, tú, él, ellos) change. Remembering this pattern helps you speak and write correctly in your routine narrative."}, {"q": "Write five connected sentences about your morning using primero, luego, antes de, después de and por fin, including at least two reflexive verbs and one stem-changing verb.", "feedback": "A model answer is: Primero me despierto a las seis. Luego me levanto y me ducho. Antes de desayunar, me visto. Después de desayunar, empiezo a leer la Biblia. Por fin, salgo para la escuela. Check that antes de and después de are followed by an infinitive, that each reflexive verb has its pronoun, and that the verbs agree with yo."}, {"q": "Explain in your own words why Reina's translation from Hebrew and Greek mattered to Spanish-speaking families, and connect it to the discussion question: why does hearing God's Word in your own language change how you pray and live every morning?", "feedback": "A strong answer explains that a translation from the original languages put the whole Bible within reach of ordinary readers in their own idioma, so they could read, memorize and pray the Scriptures with understanding. When Scripture is heard in the language of the heart, daily habits such as prayer and reading become personal rather than distant. You may also mention that translators like Reina accepted exile and hardship so that others could read, which invites us to value our own morning time in the Word."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence uses a reflexive verb correctly?", "opts": [{"t": "Mi hermana se ducha antes de desayunar.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Mi hermana ducha antes de desayunar.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Mi hermana se ducha antes de desayuna.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which is the correct yo form of 'dormir' in the present tense?", "opts": [{"t": "Dormo", "ok": false}, {"t": "Duermo", "ok": true}, {"t": "Dormio", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "In the sentence 'Después de estudiar, juego', what form must follow 'después de'?", "opts": [{"t": "A conjugated yo form", "ok": false}, {"t": "A past participle", "ok": false}, {"t": "An infinitive", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Why is Reina's 1569 Bible called the Biblia del Oso?", "opts": [{"t": "The printer's emblem on the title page shows a bear.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Reina was born in a town named Oso.", "ok": false}, {"t": "It was first read aloud in a place called the Bear Forest.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What made Reina's translation especially significant in Spanish Bible history?", "opts": [{"t": "It was the first book ever printed in Spanish.", "ok": false}, {"t": "It is generally recognized as the first complete Spanish Bible printed from the Hebrew and Greek texts.", "ok": true}, {"t": "It was a Spanish translation made only from the Latin Vulgate.", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Casiodoro de Reina's birth year (about 1520) and place of birth; confirm the wording 'born in Spain around 1520'.", "Date of the departure of the San Isidoro del Campo monks from Spain (around 1557).", "Number of years Reina spent on the translation ('roughly a dozen years') and the cities of his exile (Germany, England, Switzerland).", "Claim that the 1569 Basel Bible is 'generally recognized as the first complete Spanish Bible printed from the Hebrew and Greek texts'; check how scholars qualify it (earlier medieval and Jewish Spanish versions; use of the Ferrara Bible and Latin versions).", "Bear emblem on the title page (printer's emblem of Basel); confirm printer attribution if cited.", "Dates of the Catholic Spanish Bibles of Felipe Scío (late 18th century) and Félix Torres Amat (early 19th century).", "Customary meal times in Spain and Mexico (general cultural description)."], "reading_es": "Cada mañana, muchas personas hablan de su rutina con verbos reflexivos. Yo me despierto a las seis, me levanto, me ducho y me visto. Primero desayuno con mi familia. Luego empiezo mis tareas. Después de estudiar, puedo jugar un rato. Por la noche me acuesto temprano y duermo ocho horas. En los verbos reflexivos usamos los pronombres me, te y se. En verbos como empezar, poder y dormir, la vocal cambia: empiezo, puedo, duermo. Hace mucho tiempo vivió un monje español llamado Casiodoro de Reina. Salió de España porque había peligro para quienes leían la Biblia. En otro país trabajó durante años para traducir toda la Biblia al español desde el hebreo y el griego. Se imprimió en Basilea, en Suiza, en 1569. Se llama la Biblia del Oso porque en la portada hay un oso. Gracias a este trabajo, muchas familias pudieron leer la Palabra de Dios en su propio idioma. Por eso es bonito incluir un tiempo de lectura en nuestra rutina: Me levanto, luego leo la Biblia y oro. Esa costumbre une el idioma, la historia y la fe."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Nov": {"reading": ["When you tell a friend what you did last weekend, you use the preterite, the Spanish past tense for actions that were completed. Regular -ar verbs take the endings -é, -aste, -ó, -amos, -asteis, -aron: hablé, hablaste, habló. Regular -er and -ir verbs share the endings -í, -iste, -ió, -imos, -isteis, -ieron: comí, comiste, comió; viví, viviste, vivió. Some verbs are irregular and must be learned as vocabulary. Ir and ser have the same preterite forms, fui, fuiste, fue, fuimos, fuisteis, fueron, and context tells us which verb is meant: Fui a la iglesia means 'I went to church', while Fue un día feliz means 'It was a happy day'. Hacer becomes hice, hiciste, hizo; tener becomes tuve, tuviste, tuvo; estar becomes estuve, estuviste, estuvo. Verbs that end in -car, -gar and -zar change spelling in the yo form to keep the sound: busqué, llegué, empecé.", "Time markers help the listener place events on a timeline: ayer (yesterday), anoche (last night), el sábado pasado (last Saturday), la semana pasada (last week), el mes pasado (last month), hace dos días (two days ago). A simple weekend story might sound like this: El sábado pasado me levanté temprano, hice mis tareas y fui al parque con mi familia. Por la noche comimos juntos y leímos la Biblia. To ask someone about their own past you can say ¿Qué hiciste el sábado?, ¿Adónde fuiste? or ¿Con quién estuviste?, and to ask about a group you use the ustedes forms: ¿Qué hicieron? Two more patterns will help you read historical narratives. In the third-person forms, an unstressed i between two vowels becomes y: leyó and leyeron, from leer; huyó and huyeron, from huir, 'to flee'. Verbs ending in -ducir, such as traducir, use a j in the stem and the ending -eron: tradujo and tradujeron. And escribir, a regular -ir verb, gives escribió.", "Those verbs are exactly the ones we need to tell a story from the middle of the sixteenth century. In the 1550s the city of Seville was one of the richest in Spain, a port for the trade with the Americas, and also a place where new religious ideas were discussed. Small groups of people, among them priests, nobles and merchants, read the writings of the Reformation and the Scriptures in secret. The Spanish Inquisition was a court of the Church and the Crown that investigated suspected heresy. In Seville it put many people on trial, and public ceremonies called autos de fe took place around 1559 and 1560, in which some of the accused were executed and others were punished or reconciled.", "At the monastery of San Isidoro del Campo, outside the city, a number of monks had come to read Scripture and to question the practices of their time. When the danger increased, about a dozen of them escaped. They fled abroad, traveled through Europe, and settled in cities such as Geneva, Frankfurt and London. Casiodoro de Reina is counted among them, and so is Cipriano de Valera, whom we will meet next month. In the language of this unit: los monjes huyeron de España, tradujeron la Biblia y escribieron cartas a sus amigos.", "Responsible historians remember this period with balance. The Inquisition was a real institution that caused real suffering, and Catholic leaders themselves have, in later times, acknowledged errors committed in the name of the faith. At the same time, intolerance was not limited to one side in the sixteenth century: in several Protestant territories dissenters were also punished. Spain's Catholic and Protestant histories are intertwined, and each contains stories of courage, devotion, suffering and error. For Christians today, the lesson is not to cultivate resentment but to give thanks, with humility. We can honor those who risked everything to read the Bible, respect our Catholic neighbors and fellow citizens, and defend the freedom of conscience that every person needs, since people are made in the image of God.", "This is why the narrative you write this month matters. Practicing the preterite is not only a way to say Ayer fui, comí y estudié. It is a skill that lets you tell the stories of other people truthfully, in their own words and tenses. As you share your weekend in ten sentences, add one more that expresses thanks: Ayer leí la Biblia en mi casa, y doy gracias a Dios por poder leerla libremente."], "glossary": [{"term": "el pretérito", "def": "Preterite: the Spanish past tense for completed actions with a clear beginning or end (habló, comió, fue)."}, {"term": "anoche / ayer / la semana pasada", "def": "Time markers meaning 'last night', 'yesterday' and 'last week'; they signal that an action belongs in the preterite."}, {"term": "huir (huyeron)", "def": "To flee or escape; its third-person plural preterite is huyeron, with y between vowels."}, {"term": "traducir (tradujeron)", "def": "To translate; verbs ending in -ducir use the j-stem in the preterite (tradujo, tradujeron)."}, {"term": "la Inquisición", "def": "The Inquisition: a church-and-state court that investigated and punished suspected heresy; in Spain it operated from the late 1400s into the 1800s."}, {"term": "el auto de fe", "def": "Auto de fe: a public ceremony in which the Inquisition announced and carried out the sentences of those it had tried."}, {"term": "los reformados de Sevilla", "def": "The Seville reformers: small groups of people who read Scripture and Reformation writings in secret in the mid-1500s."}, {"term": "el exilio", "def": "Exile: living outside one's homeland because staying is dangerous or forbidden."}], "practice": [{"q": "Complete each sentence with the correct preterite form: Ayer yo (comer) ___ con mis abuelos. Anoche tú (hacer) ___ las tareas. Los monjes (huir) ___ de España. Ella (ir) ___ a la iglesia el domingo.", "feedback": "The answers are comí, hiciste, huyeron and fue (or fue as the form of ir). Comí is the regular -er yo ending -í. Hiciste is the irregular stem hic- with -iste. Huyeron changes i to y between vowels. Fue is shared by ir and ser, and here it means 'went' because of the destination a la iglesia."}, {"q": "Explain how you decide whether 'fue' means 'went' or 'was', using two example sentences.", "feedback": "Ir and ser have identical preterite forms, so context decides. If a place follows with a, as in Fue a la biblioteca, it means 'went'. If a description, time or event follows, as in Fue una reunión muy larga, it means 'was'. Reading the whole sentence for clues is a key strategy for historical texts."}, {"q": "In your own words, summarize what happened to the Seville reformers and why the monks of San Isidoro del Campo left Spain. Use at least three preterite verbs.", "feedback": "A good summary says that in the mid-1500s small groups in Seville read Scripture and Reformation writings in secret, that the Inquisition tried many of them and executed some, and that a group of monks left the monastery and fled to other countries. Sample sentences: Los monjes leyeron la Biblia, huyeron de España y tradujeron textos. Check that each verb ends in the correct preterite ending and that you avoid presenting a one-sided story."}, {"q": "How can we remember painful church history honestly and still love Catholic and Protestant neighbors today? Give two concrete attitudes or actions.", "feedback": "Good answers mention telling the truth about suffering without exaggerating or blaming present-day neighbors for past events, and acknowledging that mistakes and courage appear on many sides. Two concrete actions are listening to a neighbor's story before sharing our own and defending freedom of conscience for everyone. Jesus' command to love our neighbor, together with the idea that every person bears God's image, gives the basis for this approach."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What is the correct preterite form of 'hablar' for 'ella'?", "opts": [{"t": "hablaba", "ok": false}, {"t": "habló", "ok": true}, {"t": "hablé", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which form means 'they fled'?", "opts": [{"t": "huían", "ok": false}, {"t": "huyó", "ok": false}, {"t": "huyeron", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly says 'Last Saturday I went to the park'?", "opts": [{"t": "El sábado pasado fui al parque.", "ok": true}, {"t": "El sábado pasado voy al parque.", "ok": false}, {"t": "El sábado pasado fue al parque.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why do we write 'tradujeron' and not 'traducieron'?", "opts": [{"t": "Because tradujeron is a regular -ar verb.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Verbs ending in -ducir use a j-stem and the ending -eron in the preterite.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Because it is a present-tense form.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which statement best reflects a balanced way of remembering the Seville reformers and the Inquisition?", "opts": [{"t": "The Inquisition was the only institution in history that ever punished people for their beliefs.", "ok": false}, {"t": "The story proves that Spanish Catholics today are responsible for past actions.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Both Catholic and Protestant histories contain faith, suffering and error, and we honor the victims while loving all neighbors.", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Whether to add named figures (Rodrigo de Valer, Juan Gil, Constantino Ponce de la Fuente, Julián Hernández) in later revisions; the text now refers to the groups in general.", "Dates of the autos de fe in Seville (around 1559 and 1560) and the number of people executed (not stated in the text).", "Departure of the San Isidoro monks (about a dozen; around 1557) and the cities of exile (Geneva, Frankfurt, London).", "Statement that Catholic leaders have acknowledged errors in later times (for example, public acts of repentance in 2000); confirm wording if cited.", "Statement that dissenters were also punished in some Protestant territories in the 1500s (general historical claim).", "Whether Seville was 'one of the richest cities in Spain' in the 1550s and the role of its port in the American trade."], "reading_es": "El fin de semana pasado, yo me levanté temprano, hice mis tareas y fui al parque con mi familia. Mi mamá preparó el almuerzo y mi papá jugó con nosotros. Por la noche comimos juntos y leímos la Biblia. En español usamos el pretérito para hablar de acciones terminadas: hablé, comí, viví, fui, hice, tuve, estuve. También usamos palabras como ayer, anoche y el sábado pasado. Hace mucho tiempo, en Sevilla, algunas personas leyeron la Biblia en secreto. La Inquisición investigó a muchas de ellas y algunas sufrieron mucho. Un grupo de monjes del monasterio de San Isidoro del Campo huyó de España. Ellos viajaron por Europa, tradujeron textos y escribieron cartas. Casiodoro de Reina fue uno de estos monjes. Hoy recordamos esta historia con respeto, porque hubo dolor y errores en muchos lados. Amamos a nuestros vecinos católicos y protestantes, y damos gracias a Dios por poder leer la Biblia en libertad."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Dic": {"reading": ["Christmas is one of the best occasions to practice the imperfect tense, because holidays are full of habits and memories. The imperfect (el imperfecto) describes what used to happen, what was going on, and what things were like. For -ar verbs the endings are -aba, -abas, -aba, -ábamos, -aban: jugaba, jugábamos. For -er and -ir verbs they are -ía, -ías, -ía, -íamos, -ían: comía, vivíamos. Only three verbs are irregular: ser (era, eras, era, éramos, eran), ir (iba, ibas, iba, íbamos, iban) and ver (veía, veías, veía, veíamos, veían). Typical phrases are Cuando era pequeño, jugaba con mis primos, or Todos los años íbamos a la casa de los abuelos. Words such as siempre, todos los días, a menudo, cada Navidad and muchas veces often signal the imperfect.", "The preterite and the imperfect work together to tell a story. The imperfect sets the scene and the preterite moves the plot forward. In Era Navidad cuando llegaron los abuelos, the imperfect era tells us what the situation was, and the preterite llegaron tells us what happened at a specific moment. If you can ask yourself 'Was this a background description or habit, or a completed event?', you will usually choose the correct tense.", "The Christmas season has different customs around the Spanish-speaking world. In many countries families gather on Nochebuena, December 24, for a late dinner and sometimes for a midnight service. In Mexico and Central America people prepare tamales and attend posadas, in Puerto Rico and Venezuela groups sing during parrandas or aguinaldos, and in Spain children traditionally receive gifts on January 6, the Día de Reyes, when the Three Kings are remembered. The word aguinaldo may refer to a Christmas gift, a bonus or a carol, depending on the country, so listen to the context.", "For many evangelical families, one tradition has been the reading of Luke 2 on Christmas Eve. The text says that shepherds were keeping watch over their flock at night when an angel announced good news of great joy for all people. Notice how the narrative moves between description and event, much like the Spanish tenses we are studying: the shepherds were guarding their sheep, and then the angel appeared. In a Spanish retelling you could write Los pastores cuidaban sus ovejas cuando apareció un ángel, using the imperfect for the background and the preterite for the surprise.", "The Spanish in which many families heard that story came largely from the Reina-Valera Bible. We saw in October that Casiodoro de Reina published the first complete Spanish Bible from the original languages in Basel in 1569. Cipriano de Valera was another Spanish Reformed writer from the monastery of San Isidoro del Campo, and, like Reina, he lived in exile in Protestant Europe, spending many years in England. He spent about twenty years working on a revision of Reina's text, comparing it with the Hebrew, Greek and other versions and correcting it carefully, and it was printed in Amsterdam in 1602.", "This is why the Bible is called the Reina-Valera, abbreviated RV. Over the centuries editions of this text were revised many times, including important revisions in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, and several modern versions in Spanish are available. Each revision tries to keep the beauty of the text and also reflect advances in scholarship and language. In many Spanish-speaking evangelical homes, parents and grandparents have kept a worn RV Bible, and children have learned to read by following Luke's account of the shepherds.", "The discussion question for this unit asks what it means for a family to hand down both traditions and the Word of God. Traditions without the Word can lose their meaning, and the Word without lived traditions may not reach the children. Valera's work reminds us that someone has to take care of the text so that the next generation can read it. As you present your own family tradition, speak about it in the imperfect, Cuando era pequeño, siempre leíamos..., and ask yourself how you can pass on what you have received. Our project step 3 for La brecha invites you to draft a gap diagnosis: who needs access, where they are, and what they need. Handing something down always begins with noticing who is missing it."], "glossary": [{"term": "el imperfecto", "def": "Imperfect: the Spanish past tense for habits, descriptions and actions in progress without a defined end (jugaba, era, íbamos)."}, {"term": "Nochebuena", "def": "Christmas Eve, December 24, celebrated in many Spanish-speaking countries with a family dinner and often a worship service."}, {"term": "el Día de Reyes", "def": "Epiphany or Three Kings' Day, January 6, a traditional day for gifts in Spain and other countries."}, {"term": "la parranda / el aguinaldo", "def": "Parranda: a festive group that sings from house to house; aguinaldo: a Christmas gift, bonus or carol, depending on the country."}, {"term": "la revisión", "def": "Revision: a careful review of a text in order to correct and improve it, as Valera did with Reina's translation."}, {"term": "la Reina-Valera (RV)", "def": "The Spanish Bible tradition that began with Reina's 1569 translation and Valera's 1602 revision, updated in later editions."}, {"term": "mientras", "def": "While; a conjunction used with the imperfect to describe two simultaneous background actions."}, {"term": "generación", "def": "Generation: people born and living at about the same time; also the passing of something from parents to children."}], "practice": [{"q": "Change these sentences from present to imperfect: Mi familia celebra la Navidad. Yo voy a casa de mis abuelos. Mi tío es muy gracioso.", "feedback": "The answers are Mi familia celebraba la Navidad; Yo iba a casa de mis abuelos; Mi tío era muy gracioso. Celebrar is a regular -ar verb, so it takes -aba. Ir and ser are irregular, so iba and era must be memorized. The imperfect shows that these were habits or descriptions in the past."}, {"q": "Choose preterite or imperfect and explain: Cuando (ser) ___ niño, (jugar) ___ con mis primos, pero un día (llegar) ___ mi tío y nos (llevar) ___ al parque.", "feedback": "The sentence is Cuando era niño, jugaba con mis primos, pero un día llegó mi tío y nos llevó al parque. The first two verbs describe a situation and a habit, so they use the imperfect. Llegó and llevó narrate specific events that happened once, so they use the preterite. The phrase un día is a clue for a single event."}, {"q": "Retell Luke 2:8-11 in three Spanish sentences, using the imperfect for the background and the preterite for the events.", "feedback": "A model answer is: Los pastores cuidaban sus ovejas por la noche. De repente apareció un ángel. El ángel dijo que había buenas noticias para todo el pueblo. The first sentence is a description, so it uses the imperfect. The next ones use appeared and said as events in the preterite, and había in the third sentence is imperfect since it describes the situation announced."}, {"q": "What does it mean for a family to hand down both traditions and the Word of God? Mention Valera's work and one tradition in your own family.", "feedback": "A thoughtful answer explains that traditions help keep memory and community alive, while the Word gives them meaning. Valera revised Reina's Bible in 1602 so that later generations could read a text of reliable quality in Spanish. You may describe a family practice, such as reading Luke 2 on Nochebuena, as a way to keep the tradition rooted in Scripture rather than only in custom."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which verb is irregular in the imperfect?", "opts": [{"t": "hablar (hablaba)", "ok": false}, {"t": "comer (comía)", "ok": false}, {"t": "ir (iba)", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence uses both tenses correctly?", "opts": [{"t": "Era Navidad cuando llegaron los abuelos.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Fue Navidad cuando llegaban los abuelos.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Era Navidad cuando llegan los abuelos.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What clue usually signals the imperfect?", "opts": [{"t": "Ayer", "ok": false}, {"t": "Todos los años", "ok": true}, {"t": "De repente", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "When was Cipriano de Valera's revision of Reina's Bible printed, and where?", "opts": [{"t": "In Basel in 1569.", "ok": false}, {"t": "In Seville in 1550.", "ok": false}, {"t": "In Amsterdam in 1602.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What is the main idea of the Reina-Valera tradition?", "opts": [{"t": "A Spanish Bible text revised and handed down through many generations.", "ok": true}, {"t": "A single Spanish Bible that has never been changed since 1569.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Bible written for use only in churches in Spain.", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Cipriano de Valera's life details: birth around 1531, years spent in England, and 'about twenty years' of work on the revision (commonly cited figure).", "Publication of the Amsterdam Bible of 1602 (and, if added later, Valera's London New Testament of 1596).", "Dates and names of major RV revisions in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.", "Regional customs: parrandas in Puerto Rico and Venezuela, tamales and posadas in Mexico and Central America, Día de Reyes in Spain; meaning of aguinaldo by country.", "Scripture: Luke 2:8-11 paraphrased, not quoted."], "reading_es": "Cuando yo era pequeño, mi familia celebraba la Navidad en la casa de los abuelos. Todos los años íbamos el 24 de diciembre. Mi abuela preparaba tamales y mis primos jugaban en el patio. Mi abuelo leía la historia de los pastores en Lucas 2. Era una noche especial. En otros países hay otras costumbres. En España, los niños reciben regalos el Día de Reyes, el 6 de enero. En Puerto Rico, la gente canta en las parrandas. Usamos el imperfecto para hablar de costumbres y descripciones: jugaba, comíamos, era. Usamos el pretérito para acciones terminadas: llegaron los abuelos. Cipriano de Valera fue un escritor español que vivió en el exilio. Revisó la Biblia de Casiodoro de Reina, y esa revisión se imprimió en Ámsterdam en 1602. Así nació la tradición de la Reina-Valera. Muchas familias han leído esa Biblia generación tras generación. Las tradiciones y la Palabra de Dios son un tesoro para compartir."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Feb": {"reading": ["When you are sick in a Spanish-speaking country, communication can make a real difference. To describe pain, Spanish uses the verb doler, which works like gustar: the thing that hurts is the subject. We say Me duele la cabeza (my head hurts) with a singular noun and Me duelen los oídos (my ears hurt) with a plural noun. Add the pronoun of the person to say who feels it: Le duele el estómago a mi hermano. To describe symptoms we often use tener: Tengo fiebre, Tengo tos, Tengo gripe, Tengo dolor de garganta. Doctors ask ¿Qué le pasa?, ¿Desde cuándo tiene los síntomas? and ¿Tiene alergias?, using usted, the polite form. Patients answer with Necesito una cita or Me siento mal desde ayer.", "Giving advice is the second skill of this unit. Spanish speakers use several patterns, each with a slightly different force. Debes descansar (you should rest) is a gentle recommendation. Tienes que beber agua (you have to drink water) is stronger. Te recomiendo tomar esta medicina uses the verb recomendar followed by an infinitive. For direct instructions, informal commands for tú are used with friends and family: Toma esta pastilla, Bebe mucha agua, Descansa. Formal commands for usted, with an -e ending for -ar verbs and an -a ending for -er and -ir verbs, are used with a doctor or pharmacist: Tome esta medicina, Beba agua, Descanse.", "In a pharmacy, the dialogue is often brief and practical. The pharmacist might ask ¿En qué puedo ayudarle?, and the customer answers Necesito algo para la tos. Read the label carefully, and ask whether the medicine needs a prescription. Reading skills therefore protect health: a person who cannot read a label cannot follow an instruction safely. This simple fact helps us understand the work of a man who lived in the early nineteenth century and who believed that learning to read was an act of love.", "Diego Thomson, whose name in English was James Thomson, was a Scottish educator born in 1788. He worked as an agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society, an organization founded in 1804 to translate, publish and distribute the Scriptures at low cost. He also used a teaching system created by the English educator Joseph Lancaster, known as the Lancasterian or mutual-instruction method. In this approach, a teacher trained older or more advanced pupils, called monitors, who then taught small groups of younger children. It allowed one teacher to instruct a large number of students with few resources, and it was well suited to places where trained teachers and schools were scarce.", "Thomson traveled in the early 1800s through parts of Spanish America. He arrived in Buenos Aires around 1818 and founded schools there. In the following years he worked in Chile, Peru and Colombia, and in several of these countries leaders of the new independent states welcomed his educational plans because they needed citizens who could read. He also carried Scripture and used it as a reading text in the schools. Because Thomson often worked with Catholic officials and communities, he sometimes used Spanish translations that were already accepted by Catholic readers. His work took place in the middle of the independence movements, when new nations were asking what kind of society they wanted to build, and many thought that education was the key to a free republic.", "Historians agree on the combination at the heart of his work: teaching people to read and putting the Bible in their hands. These two actions are connected. A person who can read can study Scripture, can understand a medicine label or a contract, and can take part in public life. In that sense literacy is a form of mercy, and so is health care. Jesus taught the crowds and healed the sick, and the Christian tradition has always seen care for the body and care for the mind and soul as parts of one love for the whole person made in God's image.", "The discussion question for this month asks why early missionaries paired teaching people to read with giving them a Bible. As you practice phrases such as Me duele, Debes descansar and Tome esta medicina, think about what you are really learning: the language of care. A flyer that offers a free resource, which is your Q2 project step 1, should therefore be written with the same care. Choose a clear title and a two-sentence message in respectful Spanish, saying what you offer, who it is for and how to receive it."], "glossary": [{"term": "doler (me duele / me duelen)", "def": "To hurt; used like gustar, with singular or plural nouns (Me duele la cabeza, Me duelen los pies)."}, {"term": "tener fiebre / tos / gripe", "def": "To have a fever / cough / flu; common expressions for describing symptoms with tener."}, {"term": "el mandato", "def": "Command: a verb form used to give instructions (informal: toma, bebe; formal: tome, beba)."}, {"term": "la receta", "def": "Prescription (and also 'recipe'): a doctor's written order for medicine."}, {"term": "el colportor / el agente de la sociedad bíblica", "def": "Agent of a Bible society: a worker who distributed Scripture; here, Thomson as an agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society."}, {"term": "escuela lancasteriana", "def": "Lancasterian school: a school using mutual instruction, in which advanced pupils (monitors) help teach younger pupils."}, {"term": "la alfabetización", "def": "Literacy education: teaching people to read and write."}, {"term": "la salud", "def": "Health: physical and mental well-being; also used in the toast and greeting '¡Salud!'."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write three sentences describing symptoms and three pieces of advice, using at least one plural 'duelen', one 'tener' expression and one formal command.", "feedback": "A model answer: Me duelen los oídos. Tengo fiebre y tos. Me duele la garganta. Doctor, ¿qué debo hacer? You may answer Descansa y bebe agua if you are speaking with a friend, or Descanse y beba agua with a doctor. Check that duelen matches a plural noun and that formal commands use the usted endings -e or -a."}, {"q": "Convert these informal commands into formal commands: Toma la medicina. Bebe agua. Descansa en casa.", "feedback": "The formal forms are Tome la medicina, Beba agua and Descanse en casa. For -ar verbs the formal command ends in -e; for -er and -ir verbs it ends in -a. The informal forms for tú often look like the él form of the present: toma, bebe, descansa."}, {"q": "Explain how a Lancasterian school worked and why it was useful in early nineteenth-century Spanish America.", "feedback": "In a Lancasterian school, a single teacher trained older pupils called monitors, and the monitors taught smaller groups of younger children. This allowed many students to learn with few teachers and little money. In a region where schools were scarce and new nations wanted literate citizens, it was a practical way to expand education. A complete answer also mentions that Thomson used Scripture as a reading text."}, {"q": "Why did early missionaries like Thomson pair teaching people to read with giving them a Bible? Connect your answer to the idea that health and literacy are both ways to care for people.", "feedback": "A good response says that a Bible is of little use to someone who cannot read, so teaching literacy and distributing Scripture belong together. Literacy also helps people with daily needs, such as reading medicine labels, so it can be seen as a form of care like health service. Both reflect the Christian conviction that every person is valued by God and deserves dignity, knowledge and help."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence is correct for 'My ears hurt'?", "opts": [{"t": "Me duelen los oídos.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Me duele los oídos.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Me dolen los oídos.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which is the formal command for 'Take this medicine'?", "opts": [{"t": "Toma esta medicina.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Tome esta medicina.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Tomas esta medicina.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Who was Diego (James) Thomson?", "opts": [{"t": "A Spanish monk who translated the Bible in Basel.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Catholic missionary of the Jesuit order.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Scottish educator and agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What was the main feature of Lancasterian schools?", "opts": [{"t": "Older pupils helped teach younger pupils.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Each class had one teacher for every five students.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Lessons were limited to religious songs.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which phrase expresses a strong obligation?", "opts": [{"t": "Te recomiendo beber agua.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Tienes que beber agua.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Podrías beber agua.", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Thomson's birth year (1788), arrival in Buenos Aires (about 1818) and the sequence of his work in Chile, Peru and Colombia, including dates and invitations from national leaders.", "Statement that Thomson sometimes used Spanish translations accepted by Catholic readers (for example, the versions of Scío or Torres Amat); confirm which texts he used.", "Joseph Lancaster's method and its description (monitors); confirm that it was used in Spanish American schools in the 1810s-1820s.", "Founding date of the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804).", "Medical dialogue phrases and regional use of tú and usted (general language guidance)."], "reading_es": "Cuando estás enfermo, necesitas explicar qué te pasa. Puedes decir: Me duele la cabeza, Me duelen los oídos, Tengo fiebre, Tengo tos y Tengo gripe. El médico pregunta: ¿Qué le pasa? ¿Tiene alergias? Después te da consejos: Debes descansar, Tienes que beber agua y Tome esta medicina. En la farmacia dices: Necesito algo para la tos. Leer bien una receta o una etiqueta ayuda a cuidar la salud. Hace casi doscientos años, un maestro escocés llamado Diego Thomson viajó por Argentina, Chile, Perú y Colombia. Él fundó escuelas donde los alumnos mayores ayudaban a enseñar a los más pequeños. También llevaba la Biblia, porque quería que la gente supiera leer y pudiera entender la Palabra de Dios. Enseñar a leer y cuidar la salud son maneras de mostrar amor. Jesús enseñó a las personas y sanó a los enfermos. Por eso, cuando usamos el español para ayudar a otros, practicamos una lengua de cuidado."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Mar": {"reading": ["Shopping is an excellent place to practice many language skills at once. In a store, un mercado or una tienda, you ask for items, compare prices, try on clothes and pay. Useful vocabulary includes el precio, barato (cheap or inexpensive), caro (expensive), la talla (clothing size), probarse (to try on, a reflexive stem-changer: me pruebo), la oferta (sale) and pagar en efectivo or con tarjeta. A typical dialogue goes: ¿Cuánto cuesta esta camisa? Cuesta veinte euros. ¿Puedo probármela? Sí, el probador está allí. Me queda un poco grande; ¿tiene una talla menor? Notice the object pronoun at the end of probármela, because la replaces la camisa and it attaches to the infinitive.", "Demonstratives point out things according to distance. Este, esta, estos and estas mean 'this' and 'these' near the speaker; ese, esa, esos and esas mean 'that' and 'those' near the listener; aquel, aquella, aquellos and aquellas mean 'that over there', far from both. Esta camisa is on my hand, esa camisa is in your hands, and aquella camisa is on the far shelf. Direct object pronouns replace a noun that receives the action to avoid repetition: lo (masculine singular), la (feminine singular), los (masculine plural) and las (feminine plural). ¿Compras el abrigo? Sí, lo compro. ¿Necesitas las botas? Sí, las necesito. The pronoun goes before a conjugated verb or after an infinitive: Lo voy a comprar and Voy a comprarlo are both correct.", "Comparisons let us weigh options. Use más ... que for 'more than', menos ... que for 'less than' and tan ... como for 'as ... as': Esta chaqueta es más cara que aquella, y esa es tan bonita como esta. The words mejor and peor mean 'better' and 'worse', and with the article they become superlatives: el mejor precio, la peor oferta.", "The theme of buying and selling also shaped one of the most interesting chapters in the spread of the Scriptures. In 1804 a group of Christians in London founded the British and Foreign Bible Society to translate and distribute the Bible at a price ordinary people could pay. Twelve years later, in 1816, the American Bible Society was founded with a similar aim. These societies employed agents and also colporteurs, from the French colporter, 'to carry on the shoulder'. The colporteur, called colportor in Spanish, was a traveling bookseller who carried Bibles, New Testaments, Gospels and tracts through towns and countryside, selling them at a very low price or giving them away when people could not pay.", "Why sell instead of simply giving? The societies understood that books given away carelessly are often not valued, but books that are priced too high are out of reach. The model aimed for a balance: prices were kept low, often below the cost of production, so that people of modest means could own a Bible, while donors covered the difference. This is a lesson in stewardship: resources should be used wisely, and God's Word should be both affordable and available.", "In the 1800s colporteurs and Bible-society agents carried Scripture through parts of Spanish America and also into Spain. One of the best-known figures was George Borrow, an English writer who traveled in Spain in the 1830s distributing the New Testament for the British and Foreign Bible Society; he was briefly imprisoned in Madrid and later wrote a lively account of his travels, The Bible in Spain. In Spain, as in other countries, the work often met resistance from authorities, and Catholic and Protestant leaders sometimes argued about the right translations and the role of tracts. Today we can read this history honestly, remembering both the sacrifices of the colporteurs and the legitimate concerns of those who feared that Scripture was being separated from the guidance of the Church.", "You will compare a modern shop with the work of a colporteur. A shop uses a fixed place, a price tag and advertising, while a colporteur went to the customer, carried the product on foot or by mule and often depended on personal conversation. Your sales-pitch dialogue should keep to the spirit of the colportor: be honest, polite and clear, never pushy. In Spanish you might say Este libro es más barato que otros, y lo puede leer toda la familia. For Q2 project step 2, ask which format best serves your community: a printed folleto, a short video or an in-person taller. Choose the format that makes the message easy to receive, and always remember the person behind the purchase."], "glossary": [{"term": "el colportor", "def": "Colporteur: a traveling seller or distributor of Bibles and religious books, who often sold them at low prices or gave them away."}, {"term": "la sociedad bíblica", "def": "Bible society: an organization that translates, prints and distributes the Scriptures, such as the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804)."}, {"term": "el folleto / el tratado", "def": "Brochure or tract: a short printed text used to share a message or teaching."}, {"term": "este, ese, aquel", "def": "Demonstratives meaning 'this, that, that over there', based on the distance from the speaker and the listener."}, {"term": "los pronombres de objeto directo", "def": "Direct object pronouns (lo, la, los, las): words that replace the noun that receives the action of the verb."}, {"term": "más ... que / menos ... que / tan ... como", "def": "Comparative structures meaning 'more than', 'less than' and 'as ... as'."}, {"term": "la talla", "def": "Size (of clothing); also 'height' in some contexts."}, {"term": "la mayordomía", "def": "Stewardship: the responsible management of resources, time and gifts entrusted by God."}], "practice": [{"q": "Answer with a direct object pronoun: ¿Compras la camisa? ¿Necesitas los zapatos? ¿Vas a pagar la cuenta?", "feedback": "The answers are Sí, la compro; Sí, los necesito; and Sí, la voy a pagar or Sí, voy a pagarla. The pronoun must match the gender and number of the noun it replaces: camisa is feminine singular, zapatos masculine plural, cuenta feminine singular. In the last sentence it may go before ir or attach to the infinitive."}, {"q": "Compare two items using más ... que, menos ... que and tan ... como. Explain your choice with 'porque'.", "feedback": "A model answer: Esta chaqueta es más cara que aquella, pero es tan bonita como la otra. Prefiero aquella porque es más barata. Check that the adjective agrees in gender and number with the noun, and that the comparative is followed by que or como as appropriate."}, {"q": "Explain how the Bible societies and colporteurs made Bibles affordable and why this mattered to ordinary people.", "feedback": "A complete answer notes that the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804) and the American Bible Society (1816) printed Scripture in large quantities, set low prices, sometimes below production cost, and used donations to cover the difference. Colporteurs carried books to people who lived far from cities and did not have access to booksellers. This mattered because many families could not otherwise own a Bible."}, {"q": "What does stewardship teach about making God's Word affordable and available? Mention one benefit and one possible challenge of the colporteur method.", "feedback": "Stewardship teaches responsible use of resources so that the Word reaches as many people as possible. One benefit is that people of modest means could own a book and hear Scripture in conversation; a challenge is that conflicts with local religious authorities, or the limits of travel and funding, sometimes hindered the work. Honest history also reminds us to listen to those who disagreed with the methods."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence uses the correct object pronoun?", "opts": [{"t": "Necesito las botas: lo compro.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Necesito las botas: las compro.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Necesito las botas: la compro.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does 'aquella chaqueta' indicate?", "opts": [{"t": "A jacket near the speaker.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A jacket that belongs to the listener.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A jacket far from both speaker and listener.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What was a colporteur?", "opts": [{"t": "A traveling seller or distributor of Bibles and tracts.", "ok": true}, {"t": "A bishop who supervised translations.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A teacher in a Lancasterian school.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "In what years were the British and Foreign Bible Society and the American Bible Society founded?", "opts": [{"t": "1569 and 1602", "ok": false}, {"t": "1804 and 1816", "ok": true}, {"t": "1818 and 1992", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why were Bibles sold at low prices rather than simply given away or sold at full cost?", "opts": [{"t": "Because printing was free in the nineteenth century.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because only wealthy people needed Bibles.", "ok": false}, {"t": "To keep them available to people of modest means while still valuing the books.", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Founding dates of the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804) and American Bible Society (1816).", "Statement that prices were kept low, sometimes below production cost, with donors covering the difference (general description of the model; confirm).", "George Borrow: activities in Spain in the 1830s, brief imprisonment in Madrid and the book The Bible in Spain (published in 1843).", "Etymology of 'colporteur' (from French 'comporter' / 'colporter'); confirm the description 'to carry on the shoulder'.", "Role of colporteurs in Spanish America in the 1800s and cases of resistance by authorities."], "reading_es": "En una tienda puedes hablar con el vendedor y comparar precios. Dices: Esta camisa es más barata que aquella. Esos zapatos son más caros, pero son mejores. Necesito una talla mediana. ¿Puedo probármela? Quiero pagar en efectivo. Para no repetir palabras usamos lo, la, los y las: Lo compro, La necesito. Hace unos doscientos años existía un trabajo especial: el colportor. Era una persona que viajaba con libros y los vendía a precios muy bajos o los regalaba. En 1804 se fundó en Londres la Sociedad Bíblica Británica y Extranjera, y en 1816, la Sociedad Bíblica Americana. Estos grupos querían que personas con pocos recursos pudieran tener una Biblia. Los colportores caminaban por pueblos y campos de España y América. Les hablaban a las familias y les leían trozos de la Biblia. Cuidar el precio de un libro es una forma de cuidar a las personas. La Palabra de Dios debe estar disponible para todos y a un precio justo."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Abr": {"reading": ["Travel opens the door to the Spanish-speaking world, and it requires its own vocabulary. At the airport, el aeropuerto, you show el pasaporte, check in la maleta and look for la puerta de embarque. At the hotel, you ask for la reservación (in Spain, la reserva), say Tengo una reservación a nombre de..., and ask about the room, la habitación. A ticket is el billete in Spain and el boleto in much of Latin America, another example of how the same word changes with geography. To talk about plans, use ir a plus an infinitive: Voy a viajar a México, Vamos a visitar a mis tíos, ¿Qué vas a hacer en Lima? The present tense of ir (voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van) carries the plan, and the infinitive carries the action.", "Directions use commands. For tú, an affirmative command often looks like the third-person present form: Sigue derecho, Dobla a la izquierda, Cruza la calle. For usted, the command uses the opposite vowel: Siga derecho, Doble a la izquierda, Cruce la calle, Tome la segunda calle. Useful expressions include a la derecha, a la izquierda, derecho or recto, la esquina, la cuadra and hasta. A short dialogue sounds like this: Perdone, ¿cómo llego al hotel? Siga dos cuadras, cruce la plaza y doble a la derecha. Se lo agradezco. Formal commands for -ar verbs end in -e and for -er and -ir verbs in -a, and verbs that change their stem in the present, such as seguir, keep the change in commands: siga.", "Travel is also an old theme in the history of the church. From the beginning of Christianity, believers have crossed seas and mountains to carry the Gospel. After the voyages of Columbus, missionaries from Spain and Portugal traveled to the Americas. Catholic religious orders, among them the Franciscans, the Dominicans and the Jesuits, began work in the sixteenth century. Franciscan friars arrived in Mexico in 1524, Dominicans soon followed, and the Jesuits began their work in the Americas in the second half of the sixteenth century. Their work was complex: it took place within a colonial system that included conquest, forced labor and cultural domination, and the missionaries themselves held a range of attitudes, from defenders of the people to supporters of the colonial order.", "One of the best-known defenders of Indigenous peoples was Bartolomé de las Casas, who lived from 1484 to 1566. He first came to the Caribbean as a colonist and owner of land, but he came to see that the treatment of the Indigenous people was unjust. He became a Dominican friar and spent decades arguing before the Spanish crown that Indigenous peoples were human beings with dignity and rights, that they should be evangelized by persuasion and not by force, and that the abuses of the conquest must stop. In the middle of the sixteenth century a famous discussion in Valladolid brought him face to face with the scholar Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda over how Indigenous peoples should be treated.", "In the nineteenth century a second wave of missionary work began. After the wars of independence, many Latin American countries became more open to ideas and people from abroad. Bible-society agents, such as Diego Thomson, and Protestant missionaries began to arrive and to work for schools, Scripture distribution and congregations. In Spain, too, the nineteenth century saw the arrival of Protestant workers, often under difficult legal conditions. As with the earlier Catholic missions, this Protestant work included faithful service, sacrifice and also mistakes. An honest history recognizes both the courage and the faults.", "What can we learn for our own missions? First, respect for human dignity is not an optional extra but part of the Gospel, because every person is made in the image of God. Second, learning the language and culture of the people you serve, as the best missionaries did, is a form of love. Third, partnership with local leaders is wiser than control from outside. These principles also guide the route you will build for your community. The Q2 step 3 project asks you to design a three-stop outreach route with directions in Spanish. As you write Primero, siga hasta la esquina; luego, doble a la derecha; por último, llegue a la biblioteca, remember that a good route is not only efficient but respectful, and that the people at each stop are neighbors, not statistics."], "glossary": [{"term": "el billete / el boleto", "def": "Ticket: billete is used in Spain and boleto in much of Latin America."}, {"term": "la reservación / la reserva", "def": "Reservation: an arrangement to hold a room, seat or table; reservación is common in Latin America, reserva in Spain."}, {"term": "el mandato formal", "def": "Formal command: a verb form used to give polite instructions to usted (siga, doble, cruce, tome)."}, {"term": "ir a + infinitivo", "def": "A construction meaning 'to be going to' do something, used for near-future plans (Voy a viajar)."}, {"term": "la misión / el misionero", "def": "Mission / missionary: an effort to share the Gospel or serve others across cultural or geographic boundaries, and the person who does it."}, {"term": "la dignidad", "def": "Dignity: the inherent worth of every human being as created in God's image."}, {"term": "orden religiosa", "def": "Religious order: a community of men or women, such as Franciscans, Dominicans and Jesuits, who live under a common rule."}, {"term": "el pueblo indígena", "def": "Indigenous people: a community that lived in the Americas before the arrival of Europeans and has its own language and culture."}], "practice": [{"q": "Give directions to a hotel using four formal commands (for example: seguir, doblar, cruzar, tomar) and two direction words.", "feedback": "A model answer: Siga derecho dos cuadras, cruce la plaza, doble a la derecha y tome la primera calle a la izquierda. Formal commands for -ar verbs use -e (doble, cruce, tome) and for -er or -ir verbs use -a; seguir changes e to i (siga). Check that you used por favor or another courtesy phrase."}, {"q": "Write three sentences about your plans for a trip using ir a + infinitive and a vocabulary word from the airport or hotel.", "feedback": "For example: Voy a viajar a Guatemala con mi familia. Vamos a llegar al aeropuerto a las seis. Mi tía va a hacer la reservación del hotel. Check that ir is conjugated for the subject (voy, vamos, va) and that the next verb is in the infinitive."}, {"q": "Who was Bartolomé de las Casas and what position did he defend? Why is it important to remember that missionary history includes both faithful service and serious failures?", "feedback": "Las Casas (1484-1566) was a Dominican friar who argued that Indigenous peoples had human dignity and rights and should not be treated with violence. Remembering that missionary history includes both service and failure keeps us honest: it prevents us from idealizing or condemning whole groups of people and helps us learn from the past. It also invites humility in our own mission work."}, {"q": "How should missionaries show respect for local culture and the dignity of every person? Give three practical examples connected with language, partnership and listening.", "feedback": "Good answers might include learning the local language and customs before speaking, working under the leadership of local pastors and teachers, and listening to the needs of the community before offering help. Others might mention avoiding pressure or manipulation and treating each person as made in God's image. These actions apply directly to your project route, where your goal is to serve and not to impose."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which is the correct formal command to tell someone 'Turn left'?", "opts": [{"t": "Dobla a la izquierda.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Dobles a la izquierda.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Doble a la izquierda.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence expresses a future plan?", "opts": [{"t": "Voy a viajar a México.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Viajé a México.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Viajaba a México.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which word means 'ticket' in much of Latin America?", "opts": [{"t": "maleta", "ok": false}, {"t": "boleto", "ok": true}, {"t": "reservación", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Who was Bartolomé de las Casas?", "opts": [{"t": "A Protestant agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Jesuit translator of the Reina-Valera Bible.", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Dominican friar who defended the dignity and rights of Indigenous peoples.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which statement best summarizes the lesson of the missionary history in this unit?", "opts": [{"t": "Missionary work has included faithful service and serious failures, so we must serve with respect and humility.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Only Catholic missionaries made mistakes in the Americas.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Missionaries from every group always respected local cultures.", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Arrival of Franciscans in Mexico (1524), Dominicans and the start of the Jesuit mission in the Americas (second half of the 16th century).", "Las Casas: dates 1484-1566, his Dominican profession, the Valladolid debate with Sepúlveda (about 1550-1551) and the title of his account of the destruction of the Indies.", "Statement that nineteenth-century Protestant workers arrived in Spain under difficult legal conditions.", "Usage of billete/boleto and reserva/reservación by country (general linguistic claim).", "Balanced wording about missionaries and the colonial system (confirm with a historian if the text is published)."], "reading_es": "El próximo verano voy a viajar a México con mi familia. Primero vamos a llegar al aeropuerto con los pasaportes y las maletas. Luego vamos a tomar un taxi al hotel. Tengo una reservación a nombre de mi papá. Si me pierdo, pregunto: Perdone, ¿cómo llego a la plaza? Me dicen: Siga derecho, doble a la izquierda y cruce la calle. Para dar direcciones usamos mandatos: sigue, dobla, cruza; con usted: siga, doble, cruce. Los viajes también son parte de la historia de la iglesia. En el siglo dieciséis llegaron a América misioneros católicos, como franciscanos, dominicos y jesuitas. Bartolomé de las Casas, un fraile dominico, defendió la dignidad de los pueblos indígenas. En el siglo diecinueve llegaron también agentes de sociedades bíblicas y misioneros protestantes, como Diego Thomson. La historia de las misiones tiene servicio fiel y también errores graves. Hoy queremos aprender de todos con respeto: cada persona tiene dignidad porque Dios la creó."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:May": {"reading": ["A community runs on the work of many people. In Spanish, la policía and los bomberos respond to emergencies, el médico and la enfermera care for the sick, el cartero delivers the mail, la bibliotecaria helps readers find books, and the office of la oficina de correos handles letters and packages. To ask for help politely, use phrases such as Necesito ayuda, ¿Puede ayudarme?, ¿Podría decirme dónde está la biblioteca? and Hay una emergencia. Describe the problem briefly and clearly: Mi hermano se cayó y le duele el brazo, or Hay humo en el edificio. Politeness matters: please and thank you, por favor and muchas gracias, with usted when you address an official or someone older.", "Another useful structure is acabar de plus an infinitive, which expresses a recent action: Acabo de llegar means 'I have just arrived', Mi madre acaba de salir means 'My mother has just left', and Acabamos de hablar con el director means 'We have just spoken with the director'. Only acabar changes; the second verb stays in the infinitive. This construction is common when you give updates in an office or on the telephone. You can also practice reading bilingual notices and forms, such as a library card application or a school registration. Look for key words: nombre, dirección, teléfono, fecha de nacimiento, firma.", "The same attention to community needs appears in the story of the evangelical church in Spain. For centuries, Protestant believers were a very small minority in a country whose public life was shaped by the Catholic Church. During the period of Francisco Franco's government, from 1939 to 1975, evangelical churches faced serious restrictions, such as limits on public worship and on the use of signs. Religious freedom improved with the 1978 Constitution and the 1980 Organic Law of Religious Freedom. In 1992 the Spanish State signed a cooperation agreement with the Federation of Evangelical Religious Entities of Spain, FEREDE, which gave evangelical churches recognition in matters such as marriage, chaplaincy services, religious teaching and tax treatment.", "This history does not mean that Spaniards are anti-religious or that Catholics are opponents. Millions of Spaniards are Catholic, many practicing and many only culturally, and the Catholic Church has a long record of charity, education and public service. Our purpose is to understand and serve, not to compete.", "Chanak's mission planning uses figures attributed to FEREDE. According to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, there are approximately 4,400 evangelical places of worship in Spain. According to the same source, approximately 3,000 Spanish municipalities have no evangelical witness; that is, there is no evangelical church or group in the town. Many of these municipalities are small rural villages, part of what Spaniards call la España vaciada, the 'emptied Spain' where the population has declined as young people move to cities. These towns often lack doctors, shops, schools and public transportation, and older residents may feel isolated.", "Why does this matter for a Spanish student? A town without a local church usually also has needs that connect directly to this unit: a pharmacy that is far away, a library that is closed, a person who needs a ride to a medical appointment, or an older neighbor who needs company. Christians who want to serve respectfully will begin with listening and with practical help, and they will do it in partnership with the people who live there. As you complete Q3 step 1, you will write a gap summary with figures labeled as 'according to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, to be verified'. Use accurate language and avoid exaggerations, because a clear and honest summary is more useful than a dramatic one.", "Finally, remember the discussion question: what practical needs does a town without a local church have, and how might Christians serve respectfully? Your service guide for this month can answer part of that question. It might include the phone numbers of emergency services, the hours of the library, the steps to register at a school, and phrases such as ¿Puede ayudarme?, Acabo de llegar and Necesito una cita. In Spanish, helpfulness begins with simple words used kindly."], "glossary": [{"term": "la FEREDE", "def": "Federation of Evangelical Religious Entities of Spain: the federation that represents many evangelical churches and entities and signed the 1992 cooperation agreement with the State."}, {"term": "el acuerdo de cooperación (1992)", "def": "Cooperation agreement: a legal agreement between the Spanish State and a religious federation that regulates matters such as marriage, chaplaincies, education and taxes."}, {"term": "lugar de culto", "def": "Place of worship: a building or space where a religious community meets."}, {"term": "la España vaciada", "def": "'Emptied Spain': a common expression for rural areas that have lost population."}, {"term": "el municipio", "def": "Municipality: the smallest administrative unit of local government in Spain, such as a town or city."}, {"term": "acabar de + infinitivo", "def": "A construction meaning 'to have just done' something (Acabo de llegar)."}, {"term": "el testimonio evangélico", "def": "Evangelical witness: the presence of a church or group of believers who live and share the Christian message in a place."}, {"term": "el servicio social", "def": "Social service: practical help given to the community, such as food, tutoring, accompaniment or visits."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write three sentences with acabar de + infinitive about things that have just happened, using different subjects.", "feedback": "For example: Acabo de llegar a la biblioteca. Mi madre acaba de hablar con el médico. Acabamos de terminar la tarea. Only acabar is conjugated to match the subject; the second verb remains an infinitive. This pattern is a very natural way to give updates."}, {"q": "Write a short dialogue (4 lines) in which a person reports an emergency and asks for help politely.", "feedback": "A model dialogue: Perdone, hay una emergencia. Mi vecino se cayó y le duele la pierna. ¿Puede llamar a una ambulancia? Sí, ya llamo. ¿Dónde está? Muchas gracias por su ayuda. Check that you used polite expressions, usted forms and a clear description of the problem."}, {"q": "Explain in your own words who FEREDE is, what happened in 1992 and what the figures cited by Chanak say. Why must the figures be labeled as 'to be verified'?", "feedback": "FEREDE is the federation that represents many evangelical churches and entities in Spain. In 1992 the State signed a cooperation agreement with the federation. According to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, there are approximately 4,400 places of worship and approximately 3,000 municipalities without an evangelical witness. They are labeled 'to be verified' because they are second-hand figures that must be checked against the official source before they are used in a report or grant application."}, {"q": "What practical needs does a town without a local church have, and how might Christians serve respectfully?", "feedback": "Possible needs include access to medical care, transportation, a library or school services, and company for older people. Respectful service starts by listening, working with local people and institutions, offering help without conditions and avoiding pressure or criticism of local customs. In this way, social service becomes a visible expression of love for the neighbor."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence correctly says 'I have just arrived'?", "opts": [{"t": "Acabo de llegar.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Acabé llegar.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Estoy acabando llegar.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does FEREDE represent?", "opts": [{"t": "The Catholic bishops of Spain.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Many evangelical churches and entities in Spain.", "ok": true}, {"t": "The Spanish Ministry of Education.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "According to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak (to be verified), approximately how many Spanish municipalities have no evangelical witness?", "opts": [{"t": "About 300", "ok": false}, {"t": "About 30,000", "ok": false}, {"t": "About 3,000", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What does the expression 'la España vaciada' refer to?", "opts": [{"t": "Rural areas of Spain that have lost population.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Churches that have closed in the cities.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Public buildings without a roof.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What is the best first step in serving a town without a local church?", "opts": [{"t": "Distribute materials without talking to anyone.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Listen to the community and offer practical help in partnership with local people.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Assume that the people have no needs.", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Figure of approximately 4,400 evangelical places of worship in Spain, attributed to FEREDE and cited by Chanak: verify against the official FEREDE source and the year of the data.", "Figure of approximately 3,000 municipalities without an evangelical witness, attributed to FEREDE and cited by Chanak: verify the source, the definition of 'witness' and the date.", "1992 cooperation agreements (Laws 24/1992 for evangelicals, signed in November 1992) and their contents (marriage, chaplaincy, religious teaching, tax treatment).", "Description of restrictions on evangelical churches during Franco's government (1939-1975) and of the 1978 Constitution and 1980 Organic Law of Religious Freedom.", "Name of the federation: Federación de Entidades Religiosas Evangélicas de España.", "Use of 'la España vaciada' (a common expression) and statements about services in rural towns (general)."], "reading_es": "En una comunidad trabajan muchas personas: la policía, los bomberos, el médico, el cartero y la bibliotecaria. Cuando necesitas ayuda, dices: Necesito ayuda, ¿Puede ayudarme? o Hay una emergencia. También puedes decir: Acabo de llegar y Acabamos de hablar con el director. En España hay iglesias evangélicas, pero son una minoría pequeña. En 1992, el Estado firmó un acuerdo de cooperación con la FEREDE, la federación que representa a muchas iglesias evangélicas. Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España. Según la misma fuente, hay aproximadamente 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico, sobre todo pueblos pequeños de la llamada España vaciada. Estas cifras están por verificar. En un pueblo sin iglesia también puede faltar un médico, una farmacia o compañía para una persona mayor. Los cristianos podemos servir con respeto, escuchar primero y ayudar con acciones sencillas. El servicio al prójimo también es una forma de ministerio."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Jun": {"reading": ["What do you want to be when you grow up? In Spanish you can answer with several tools. Professions use masculine and feminine forms: el ingeniero and la ingeniera, el maestro and la maestra, el enfermero and la enfermera, el médico and la médica, and el pastor and la pastora in churches that use the title. Workplaces include la oficina, la escuela, el hospital, la iglesia and el taller. To state plans, use ir a plus infinitive: Voy a estudiar medicina. The simple future adds endings to the infinitive: seré, serás, será, seremos, serán, and for irregular verbs, tendré, haré, podré, diré. To express a polite wish or a dream, use the conditional: Me gustaría ser maestra, Me gustaría ayudar a los niños. To justify, use Creo que..., Pienso que... and Quiero ser ... porque ....", "A job interview in Spanish is a good exercise in using these structures formally with usted: ¿Por qué quiere este trabajo? Quiero este trabajo porque soy responsable y me gusta ayudar. Speak clearly and thank the interviewer at the end. As you describe your plans for five and ten years, mix the forms: En cinco años voy a terminar mis estudios; en diez años seré maestro, y me gustaría trabajar en un proyecto educativo.", "These career choices connect to a major theme in the history of Ibero-American Christianity: the growth of evangelical churches in Latin America during the twentieth century. As we saw in March and April, evangelical work in the region began with Bible-society agents, colporteurs and missionaries in the 1800s. Historic denominations such as Presbyterian, Methodist and Baptist churches established congregations and schools in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, and in 1916 a major missions conference was held in Panama to discuss Protestant work in Latin America.", "In the early twentieth century a new movement began to change the picture. Pentecostalism, which emphasizes the work of the Holy Spirit, healing and personal conversion, arrived in Latin America in the first decades of the 1900s. In Chile a revival in Valparaíso around 1909, associated with the Methodist missionary Willis Hoover, led to the formation of Pentecostal churches led by Chileans. In Brazil, missionaries and migrants began Pentecostal congregations around 1910 and 1911.", "How large is this growth? Estimates vary greatly from country to country, and there are differences in how studies define 'evangelical'. Chanak, in its mission planning, cites estimates that evangelical presence in Latin America has risen from about 2 percent historically to between 20 and 40 percent today. These are approximate figures, to be verified, and are not statistics for any single country. What we can say confidently is that evangelical Christianity in Latin America grew from a small minority to a large and visible part of religious life, though in different ways and at different speeds from one country to another.", "Growth is a blessing, and it also brings challenges. Evangelical churches belong to several families. Pentecostal churches emphasize the Spirit's gifts and experience. Baptist churches stress believer's baptism and congregational life. Reformed and Presbyterian churches value the historic confessions and the teaching of Scripture. Historic or mainline churches, such as Methodist, Lutheran and Anglican communities, carry older traditions. Rapid growth creates a great need for trained pastors, teachers and Christian educators, because a church can multiply faster than leaders can be formed. When there are few trained leaders, there is a risk of shallow teaching, of dependence on a single charismatic figure, and of believers who attend services but have not been deeply formed in the faith.", "This is the challenge of discipleship, which means going beyond attendance. Jesus' command in Matthew 28 was to make disciples, to baptize and to teach everything he commanded, not only to gather crowds. Discipleship requires patient teaching, a community of accountability, service and mature leadership. The discussion question for this month asks what it takes to move from a large number of believers to deeply formed disciples. Your own career plan is a small part of that answer. For Q3 step 2, assign roles in your outreach plan, such as maestro, voluntario, traductor and pastor, and explain in Spanish why each role matters."], "glossary": [{"term": "el futuro simple", "def": "Simple future: a verb form for what will happen, built by adding endings to the infinitive (estudiaré, seré, tendré)."}, {"term": "el condicional", "def": "Conditional: a verb form used for polite wishes and hypothetical situations (Me gustaría ser maestra)."}, {"term": "el pentecostalismo", "def": "Pentecostalism: a Christian movement emphasizing the work of the Holy Spirit, healing and personal conversion, which grew rapidly in Latin America in the 1900s."}, {"term": "las iglesias históricas", "def": "Historic churches: older Protestant denominations such as Presbyterian, Methodist, Lutheran and Anglican churches."}, {"term": "el discipulado", "def": "Discipleship: the process of learning to follow Christ through teaching, community, service and obedience."}, {"term": "la formación de líderes", "def": "Leadership training: the education of pastors, teachers and other leaders for the church."}, {"term": "la entrevista de trabajo", "def": "Job interview: a formal conversation in which an employer asks questions to evaluate a candidate."}, {"term": "una estimación", "def": "Estimate: an approximate calculation or judgment that is not an exact count."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write five sentences about your future using ir a, the simple future, 'me gustaría' and 'porque'.", "feedback": "A model answer: Voy a estudiar en la universidad. Seré enfermera. Me gustaría trabajar en un hospital. Quiero ayudar a las familias porque creo que cada persona tiene valor. En diez años tendré mi propia clínica. Check the endings of the future (-é, -ás, -á, -emos, -án) and that the conditional gustaría is used for a polite wish."}, {"q": "Prepare three questions and answers for a job interview in Spanish, using usted.", "feedback": "For example: ¿Por qué quiere este trabajo? Quiero este trabajo porque me gusta enseñar. ¿Cuáles son sus puntos fuertes? Soy puntual y responsable. ¿Qué horario prefiere? Prefiero trabajar por las mañanas. The interviewer uses usted forms (quiere, prefiere, sus), and the answers are in the first person."}, {"q": "Describe the four main families of evangelical churches mentioned in the reading and give one strength of each.", "feedback": "Pentecostal churches emphasize the Holy Spirit and have often grown through local leadership; Baptist churches emphasize believer's baptism and active congregations; Reformed and Presbyterian churches emphasize teaching and historic confessions; historic or mainline churches, such as Methodist and Lutheran, provide older traditions and educational institutions. Each has strengths and weaknesses, so a balanced answer avoids ranking them."}, {"q": "Why is it important to move from a large number of believers to deeply formed disciples? Connect your answer to the figures cited by Chanak and to Matthew 28.", "feedback": "The estimates cited by Chanak, from about 2 percent to between 20 and 40 percent, are approximate and must be verified, but they illustrate a rapid growth. Growth creates a need for trained pastors and teachers, because attendance alone does not guarantee maturity. Matthew 28 commands us to make disciples who learn to obey what Christ taught, so churches need patient teaching, accountability and service in addition to numbers."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence uses the simple future correctly?", "opts": [{"t": "Mañana estudiaba para el examen.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Mañana estudiaré para el examen.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Mañana estudio para el examen ayer.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sentence expresses a polite wish?", "opts": [{"t": "Voy ser maestra.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Fui maestra.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Me gustaría ser maestra.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What is the main idea behind the range 'from 2 percent to 20-40 percent' cited by Chanak?", "opts": [{"t": "Evangelical presence in Latin America grew significantly, though the figures are estimates that vary by country and must be verified.", "ok": true}, {"t": "The numbers are exact and apply to every Latin American country.", "ok": false}, {"t": "The Catholic Church disappeared in the region.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which movement emphasizes the work of the Holy Spirit and grew rapidly in Latin America in the 1900s?", "opts": [{"t": "Lancasterian education", "ok": false}, {"t": "Pentecostalism", "ok": true}, {"t": "Colportage", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "According to the reading, what is a major challenge for churches that grow rapidly?", "opts": [{"t": "Finding larger Bibles", "ok": false}, {"t": "Avoiding all contact with other churches", "ok": false}, {"t": "Forming trained leaders and deeply formed disciples", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Estimate attributed to Chanak: evangelical presence 'from about 2 percent historically to 20-40 percent today'; verify the source, the definition of 'evangelical', the year and the countries covered. Do not apply it to any single country.", "Chile: revival in Valparaíso around 1909 associated with Willis Hoover (Methodist missionary).", "Brazil: beginnings of Pentecostal congregations around 1910-1911.", "Panama Congress on Christian Work in Latin America (1916).", "Establishment of Presbyterian, Methodist and Baptist work in Latin America in the 1800s and early 1900s (dates vary by country).", "Description of denominational families (Pentecostal, Baptist, Reformed, historic) as a general classification."], "reading_es": "¿Qué quieres ser cuando seas mayor? Puedes decir: Voy a estudiar medicina. Seré maestra. Me gustaría ser ingeniero porque creo que puedo ayudar a muchas personas. También puedes imaginar una entrevista: ¿Por qué quiere este trabajo? Quiero este trabajo porque soy responsable y me gusta ayudar. En América Latina, las iglesias evangélicas crecieron mucho durante el siglo veinte, sobre todo las iglesias pentecostales. Las iglesias históricas, como presbiterianas, metodistas y bautistas, llegaron antes. Según estimaciones citadas por Chanak, la presencia evangélica pasó de un 2 % histórico a entre un 20 % y un 40 % actual. Esas cifras varían mucho según el país y están por verificar. Este crecimiento trae una gran necesidad de pastores, maestros y cristianos bien formados. Un buen discípulo no solo asiste a la iglesia, sino que aprende, sirve y vive su fe cada día. Por eso, todas las profesiones pueden ayudar: el maestro, la enfermera, el traductor, el voluntario y el pastor."}, "us:world_language:spanish2:Jul": {"reading": ["This final unit gathers the year's work into a single plan. In language terms, you now have a toolbox: reflexive verbs and stem-changers for routines, the preterite and imperfect for telling stories, commands for directions, the future and the conditional for plans, comparisons for evaluating choices, and polite requests for community life. Ministry and hospitality add their own phrases: Bienvenidos, ¿En qué puedo servirle?, Estamos para ayudarle, Con gusto, Dios le bendiga. To keep a conversation going, use clarifying phrases such as ¿Puede repetir, por favor?, ¿Cómo se dice ... en español?, Perdone, no entendí and Quiero decir que.... When you cannot find a word, use circumlocution, which means describing it with simpler words: Es una persona que trabaja en la farmacia.", "The ACTFL scale runs from Novice to Advanced, each level with Low, Mid and High sublevels, and this course aims to move you from Novice High toward Intermediate Low, where you can create with the language, ask and answer simple questions, and handle everyday situations.", "History gives meaning to these words. Let us follow the timeline of the year. In the sixteenth century Casiodoro de Reina and other monks from San Isidoro del Campo left Spain because of the Inquisition, and Reina published the Biblia del Oso in Basel in 1569. Cipriano de Valera revised it, and the revision appeared in Amsterdam in 1602, leading to the Reina-Valera tradition. In the early nineteenth century Diego Thomson combined Lancasterian schools with Scripture distribution in Spanish America, and the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804) and the American Bible Society (1816) sent colporteurs who made Bibles affordable. In the twentieth century evangelical churches, and especially Pentecostal churches, grew in Latin America; in Spain, evangelical churches gained legal recognition through the cooperation agreement of 1992.", "The same timeline allows an honest look at the present. According to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, there are approximately 4,400 evangelical places of worship in Spain and approximately 3,000 municipalities without an evangelical witness; the figures are to be verified. For Latin America, estimates cited by Chanak speak of growth from about 2 percent historically to between 20 and 40 percent today, but they vary greatly by country and also need verification. Together these facts point to two needs. One is presence: there are towns and neighborhoods where people may not have any local community of faith or service. The other is depth: where churches have grown, there is a great need for discipleship and trained leaders.", "The final project, La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar (The Gap and How to Reach It), pulls these threads together. Over the year you have built it step by step: you listed who could use a Bible or a Christian resource, wrote five questions for a Spanish-speaking neighbor, drafted a gap diagnosis, designed a flyer message, chose a format, built an outreach route, wrote a gap summary with sourced figures and assigned roles. Now you present it in Spanish. The plan should explain who the gap affects, where they are, what need they have, how you will reach them respectfully and who will do what. A strong plan connects each decision to the history you have studied: the translators who gave Spanish speakers a Bible, the teachers who paired literacy with Scripture, the colporteurs who kept prices low, and the leaders who need training today.", "The project is graded with the Chanak rubric of three parts. Knowledge counts for 30 percent: correct information about the history, accurate use of figures with their attribution, and sound grammar and vocabulary. Execution counts for 40 percent: a clear, feasible plan with a route, a format, a message and defined roles, presented in Spanish with confidence. Local Connection counts for 30 percent: real knowledge of the community you want to serve, including its Spanish-speaking neighbors, needs and existing resources.", "When we serve the Hispanic community, we need humility, clarity and love. The question for discussion asks which part of the history inspires your plan most and who in your community could it serve. Whatever your answer, and with humility and prayer, you are joining a long story of people who believed that people should hear and read the Word in their own language and be treated as bearers of God's image. In your final assessment you will show what you have learned in conversation, writing and service, and you will pray with those you serve."], "glossary": [{"term": "la brecha", "def": "The gap: the difference between the need and the present response; in this project, the lack of Spanish-language access to Scripture, a church or service in a place."}, {"term": "alcanzar", "def": "To reach; here, to reach people with a message, a resource or an act of service."}, {"term": "la hospitalidad", "def": "Hospitality: welcoming and caring for people, especially strangers or guests."}, {"term": "la circunlocución", "def": "Circumlocution: describing a word you do not know using simpler words, so that communication can continue."}, {"term": "la rúbrica", "def": "Rubric: a scoring guide that lists criteria; Chanak's project rubric weights Knowledge 30%, Execution 40% and Local Connection 30%."}, {"term": "discipulado", "def": "Discipleship: the long-term process of forming followers of Christ through teaching, community and service."}, {"term": "Novice High / Intermediate Low", "def": "Levels of the ACTFL proficiency scale; this course aims to move students from the top of the Novice level toward the beginning of the Intermediate level."}, {"term": "la línea del tiempo", "def": "Timeline: a sequence of events arranged in order of date."}], "practice": [{"q": "Create a timeline in Spanish with five events from this course, using the preterite, such as Reina publicó... or Thomson fundó....", "feedback": "A model timeline: En 1569 Reina publicó la Biblia del Oso. En 1602 Valera publicó su revisión. En 1804 se fundó la Sociedad Bíblica Británica y Extranjera. Alrededor de 1818 Thomson llegó a Buenos Aires. En 1992 el Estado firmó un acuerdo con la FEREDE. Check dates and that every verb is in the preterite."}, {"q": "Write a short conversation (6 lines) in which you welcome a Spanish-speaking neighbor, ask an open question, clarify something you did not understand and close politely.", "feedback": "A model: Bienvenida, ¿en qué puedo servirle? Busco información sobre las clases de inglés. Con gusto. ¿Cómo se dice 'schedule' en español? Se dice 'horario'. Muchas gracias; Dios le bendiga. Check that you used usted, one open question, a clarification phrase and a polite closing."}, {"q": "Summarize 'La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar' in five sentences: who, where, what need, how and who will do what. Mention one figure and attribute it correctly.", "feedback": "A good summary names a group (for example Spanish speakers in a specific neighborhood), the place, the need (for instance a Bible, tutoring or company) and the plan with roles. If you cite a figure, write 'according to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, to be verified', and do not use it as if it were official data. Connect your plan to the history you have studied to show Knowledge, and make sure it is realistic so that Execution is clear."}, {"q": "Which part of the history in this course inspires your plan most, and who in your community could it serve? Explain how the Chanak rubric would evaluate your answer.", "feedback": "There is no single right answer: you may choose Reina's translation, Thomson's schools, the colporteurs' low prices or the need for discipleship. A strong response ties the inspiration to a real group of neighbors and a concrete action. Under the rubric, the accuracy of your history and language counts for 30%, a feasible plan with roles and a format counts for 40%, and your knowledge of the local context counts for 30%."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What is the weight of each part of the Chanak rubric for the project?", "opts": [{"t": "Knowledge 40%, Execution 30%, Local Connection 30%", "ok": false}, {"t": "Knowledge 50%, Execution 25%, Local Connection 25%", "ok": false}, {"t": "Knowledge 30%, Execution 40%, Local Connection 30%", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which phrase is a clarifying expression?", "opts": [{"t": "¿Puede repetir, por favor?", "ok": true}, {"t": "Bienvenidos.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Dios le bendiga.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which statement correctly attributes the figure about places of worship?", "opts": [{"t": "There are exactly 4,400 places of worship, according to the Spanish government.", "ok": false}, {"t": "According to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, there are approximately 4,400 places of worship (to be verified).", "ok": true}, {"t": "Chanak counted every place of worship in Spain.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sequence of events is in correct chronological order?", "opts": [{"t": "FEREDE agreement (1992), Reina (1569), Thomson (1800s), Valera (1602)", "ok": false}, {"t": "Thomson (1800s), Reina (1569), FEREDE agreement (1992), Valera (1602)", "ok": false}, {"t": "Reina's Bible (1569), Valera's revision (1602), Thomson's work (1800s), FEREDE agreement (1992)", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What are the two needs that the history and figures of this course point to?", "opts": [{"t": "Presence in places without a church or service and depth through discipleship and trained leaders", "ok": true}, {"t": "Large buildings and more advertising", "ok": false}, {"t": "Political influence and public pressure", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Dates of the timeline: Reina's Bible (1569), Valera's revision (1602), British and Foreign Bible Society (1804), American Bible Society (1816), FEREDE cooperation agreement (1992).", "Figures of approximately 4,400 evangelical places of worship and approximately 3,000 municipalities without an evangelical witness, attributed to FEREDE and cited by Chanak: verify source and date.", "Estimate of evangelical growth in Latin America 'from about 2 percent to 20-40 percent', as cited by Chanak: verify source and definitions; do not use for single countries.", "ACTFL proficiency levels and the course target (Novice High toward Intermediate Low); verify alignment with the official ACTFL descriptors.", "Chanak rubric weights for the project (30% / 40% / 30%) as provided by the curriculum team."], "reading_es": "Este año aprendimos mucho español. Hablamos de nuestra rutina con verbos reflexivos, contamos el pasado con el pretérito y el imperfecto, dimos direcciones con mandatos y hablamos del futuro con ir a y el futuro simple. También estudiamos historia. Casiodoro de Reina publicó la Biblia del Oso en 1569, y Cipriano de Valera la revisó en 1602. Diego Thomson fundó escuelas y repartió la Biblia. Los colportores hicieron la Biblia más barata. Hoy, según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España y aproximadamente 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico. Esas cifras están por verificar. En América Latina, el crecimiento evangélico muestra una gran necesidad de discipulado y líderes formados. Mi proyecto se llama La brecha y cómo alcanzar. Voy a explicar quién necesita ayuda, dónde está y cómo podemos servirle con respeto. Diré: Bienvenidos, ¿En qué puedo servirle? y Dios le bendiga. La rúbrica tiene tres partes: conocimiento, ejecución y conexión local."}});
